478 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1164 0 R
/E (Even fetuses 26 to 28 weeks old move in response to a sharp sound. Newborn infants turn their heads toward the source of a sound. Interestingly, the head-turning response disappears at about six weeks and does not reemerge until three or four months, at which time the infants also search with their eyes for the source of the sound. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
479 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1165 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
480 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1166 0 R
/E (It seems it is the particular rhythms of a language that infants are discerning. Newborns will not distinguish between their own language and other languages that are similar in rhythm, such as Dutch and English, but will distinguish between languages with different rhythms, such as Japanese and Polish \(Ramus, 2002\).)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
481 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Hearing \(continued\))
/E (Hearing \(continued\))
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
482 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1167 0 R
/E (Across cultures, adults speak to young infants quite differently from how they speak to older children and adults, using a higher pitch to the voice, different con tours to sentences and longer pauses between sentences. This style of speech, often referred to as babytalk or motherese, seems to be just what a baby wants and needs. Infants in the first months of life prefer motherese over normal speech, even when presented by a stranger, and motherese helps infants detect the boundaries between words \(Cooper et al., 1997; Fernald, 1985; Thiessen et al., 2005\). )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
483 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1168 0 R
/E (Infants as young as six months are also able to discriminate between intonations of voice indicating approval and disapproval, and smile more in response to approval intonations over disapproval into nations, even when presented in a language different from their own \(Fernald, 1993\). Thus, infants seem to be able to extract important meanings from the speech around them, and are especially attentive to the kind of speech most frequently directed at them.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
484 0 obj
